## Interfaces — the neighbours you must physically meet
{{ interfaces }}

Those neighbour boxes are the truth another session is building to. Where your part attaches to
one, overlap it by 2–5 mm (never leave a gap, never float); where a neighbour attaches to yours,
leave a real surface for it at the stated box — no recess it cannot reach, no shape that would
make it interpenetrate.

## Detail budget (this is what "finished" means here)
{{ detail_budget }}

## DO
- Place each part so its bounding box CENTRE and EXTENTS equal the plan row (±0.01 m).
- Build the described geometry, not a box with the right size: profiles, tapers, chamfers 2–6 mm,
  grooves, fasteners, seams, the counted features the description names (spokes, slats, dentils…).
  A part that is a scaled cube where the plan says "fluted cylindrical body with 6 flutes" fails.
- Use loops/arrays for repeated features rather than hand-placing them; parameterise from the
  constants at the top of your file so the numbers stay honest.
- Material per part with the stated colour, roughness and metalness.
- Instances (`{{ '{' }}Name{{ '}' }}_0 … _N-1`) are looped inside YOUR file, each TOP-LEVEL.

## DON'T
- Do not build, rename, move or "fix" a part that is not in your table — even if it looks wrong.
- Do not write the entry file, a README, notes or tests.
- No placeholders ("TODO", "simplified"), no unit-cube stand-ins, no part outside its plan bbox.
- No environment sniffing, no DOM/texture loading/network/file IO, no camera/light/render/export.

